  "unit": "major",
  "title": "Your 00 to 99 List",
  "short": "Most users fix one picture word for every two-digit number, 00 to 99, and learn it as a big peg list.",
  "trouble": "Changing your list words often. Fixed words become instant; changing ones slow you down.",
  "prereqs": [
    "memsys.decode-words"
  ],
  "xp": 3,
  "viz": [
    "mm-chunks"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "practice": {
    "kind": "worked-example",
    "q": "Which words stand for 1492?",
    "a": "Tire, bone: a tire rolling over a bone.",
    "steps": [
      "14: tire.",
      "92: bone."
    ],
    "title": "Worked example"
  },
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Making a word on the spot is slow. So most people who use the Major System choose <b>one picture word for each number from 00 to 99</b> and learn it until it is instant. Then 1492 is not four digits to code; it is two pictures."
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Here is a list we wrote for this course. Every word was checked against its sounds. Choose your own if another word is easier to picture, but keep each one fixed."
      },
      {
